  "title": "The 2018 Summer Box Office Slowed Again. Is Chinese Cinema About to Hit Its Ceiling?",
  "excerpt": "This time it's the audience's numbers and habits that set the industry's ceiling.",
  "body": "This time it's the audience's numbers and habits that set the industry's ceiling.\n\nFor an industry that has grown fast for more than a decade, the ceiling can arrive at any moment. Judging by the summer season that just ended, this is exactly the question Chinese cinema now has to start asking.\n\nHollywood usually dates the summer season from May — a habit *Jaws* and *Star Wars* established in the late 1970s. In China, the summer season means something else: the stretch from late June to late August, when students are out of school and have plenty of time to watch movies.\n\nQDaily tallied the box office for the past five summer seasons, counting from the last full weekend of June to the last full weekend before September. Over those 66 days, the 2018 summer season took in about 13.8 billion yuan after the theater's cut — roughly 10% more than last year, and double the figure of five years ago, in 2014.\n\nThe trouble is that in some recent years summer box office grew by more than 30%, which makes 10% look thin. And because ticket prices rose slightly, admissions grew even more slowly than revenue — only about 7%. As in 2016, growth in the film market has slowed sharply again.\n\n![](http://img.qdaily.com/uploads/20180831142422Ub8evk9xcplH1L0h.jpg-w600)\n\nThe two slowdowns have different causes, though. Two years ago the blame fell on the movies themselves. That summer's lineup included the \"epically\" bad *League of Gods* and the incoherent, badly acted *Sweet Sixteen*《夏有乔木，雅望天堂》. Even *Time Raiders*, the summer's highest-grossing film, scored only 4.7 on Douban. Paying for a ticket and getting a thoroughly unpleasant experience in return was seen as the main reason audiences stayed away from theaters.\n\nThis year the problem does not lie with the people making the movies. *Dying to Survive*, *The Island* and *Hello Mr. Billionaire* scored 9.0, 7.3 and 6.7 on Douban; even *The Meg* — a Chinese-American co-production, a category that had never produced a success before — landed right on the 6.0 passing line. When film quality is basically solid and still cannot pull more people into theaters, the conclusion is hard to avoid: Chinese cinema's ceiling is close. And this time the height of that ceiling is set by the people who watch the movies.\n\n### Have third- and fourth-tier cities hit saturation?\n\nOne of the main reasons admissions rose over the past decade is the expansion of theater chains. At the end of 2009 China had 4,723 screens; by the end of May 2018 there were 56,343, more than a tenfold increase.\n\nMany of the new theaters went up in third- and fourth-tier cities that had no cinema before, and gradually taught residents of those cities to go to the movies. [Data show](http://%E6%95%B0%E6%8D%AE%E6%98%BE%E7%A4%BA%EF%BC%8C%E4%BB%8E%202014%20%E5%B9%B4%E5%88%B0%202017%20%E5%B9%B4%EF%BC%8C%E4%B8%80%E7%BA%BF%E5%9F%8E%E5%B8%82%E8%A7%82%E5%BD%B1%E4%BA%BA%E6%AC%A1%E7%9A%84%E5%A4%8D%E5%90%88%E5%B9%B4%E5%9D%87%E5%A2%9E%E9%95%BF%E7%8E%87%E4%B8%BA%2019%25%EF%BC%8C%E8%80%8C%E4%B8%89%E7%BA%BF%E5%9F%8E%E5%B8%82%E5%92%8C%E5%9B%9B%E7%BA%BF%E5%9F%8E%E5%B8%82%E5%88%86%E5%88%AB%E8%BE%BE%E5%88%B0%E4%BA%86%2027%25%20%E5%92%8C%2035%25%E3%80%82%E7%94%B5%E5%BD%B1%E4%B8%9A%E5%9B%A0%E6%AD%A4%E4%B8%80%E5%BA%A6%E5%BC%80%E5%A7%8B%E8%BF%B7%E4%BF%A1%E4%BA%8E%E2%80%9C%E5%BE%97%E5%B0%8F%E9%95%87%E9%9D%92%E5%B9%B4%E8%80%85%E5%BE%97%E5%A4%A9%E4%B8%8B%E2%80%9D%E7%9A%84%E8%A7%82%E7%82%B9%E3%80%82%0A%0A%0A%0A%0A%0A%0A%0A%E8%BF%99%E9%80%9A%E5%B8%B8%E6%84%8F%E5%91%B3%E7%9D%80%EF%BC%8C%E4%B8%AD%E5%9B%BD%E7%94%B5%E5%BD%B1%E5%8D%B3%E5%B0%86%E5%91%8A%E5%88%AB%E9%AB%98%E9%80%9F%E5%A2%9E%E9%95%BF%E6%9C%9F%EF%BC%8C%E8%BF%9B%E5%85%A5%E4%B8%80%E4%B8%AA%E7%9B%B8%E5%AF%B9%E7%A8%B3%E5%AE%9A%E7%9A%84%E5%8F%91%E5%B1%95%E9%98%B6%E6%AE%B5%E5%BD%93%E4%B8%AD%E3%80%82%0A%0A%E4%B8%8A%E4%B8%80%E6%AC%A1%E4%B8%AD%E5%9B%BD%E7%94%B5%E5%BD%B1%E5%87%BA%E7%8E%B0%0A%0A%0A%E5%8F%96%E5%86%B3%E4%BA%8E%E7%94%B5%E5%BD%B1%E5%B1%80%E7%BB%99%E5%AE%9A%E7%9A%84%E6%A1%A3%E6%9C%9F%EF%BC%8C%E5%AE%83%E5%8F%AF%E8%83%BD%E4%BC%9A%E4%BB%A5%E4%B8%80%E9%83%A8%E5%A5%BD%E8%8E%B1%E5%9D%9E%E5%A4%A7%E7%89%87%E6%8B%89%E5%BC%80%E5%B8%B7%E5%B9%95%EF%BC%8C%E4%BE%8B%E5%A6%82%202014%20%E5%B9%B4%E5%92%8C%202017%20%E5%B9%B4%E7%9A%84%E3%80%8A%E5%8F%98%E5%BD%A2%E9%87%91%E5%88%9A%E3%80%8B%E7%B3%BB%E5%88%97%EF%BC%8C%E4%BD%86%E9%80%9A%E5%B8%B8%E6%83%85%E5%86%B5%E4%B8%8B%EF%BC%8C%E6%9A%91%E6%9C%9F%E6%A1%A3%E8%A2%AB%E7%A7%B0%E4%B8%BA%E2%80%9C%E5%9B%BD%E4%BA%A7%E4%BF%9D%E6%8A%A4%E6%9C%88%E2%80%9D%EF%BC%8C%E5%9C%A8%E8%BF%99%E6%9C%9F%E9%97%B4%EF%BC%8C%E6%9C%89%E7%AB%9E%E4%BA%89%E5%8A%9B%E7%9A%84%E8%BF%9B%E5%8F%A3%E7%89%87%E5%BE%88%E5%B0%91%E5%8F%AF%E4%BB%A5%E5%AE%9A%E6%A1%A3%E5%9C%A8%E8%BF%99%E6%9C%9F%E9%97%B4%E6%94%BE%E6%98%A0%EF%BC%8C%E8%80%8C%E5%9B%BD%E4%BA%A7%E7%89%87%E5%B0%B1%E8%83%BD%E5%A4%9F%E5%88%A9%E7%94%A8%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%9F%E6%94%BE%E6%9A%91%E5%81%87%E7%9A%84%E6%9C%BA%E4%BC%9A%EF%BC%8C%E8%8E%B7%E5%BE%97%E6%9B%B4%E9%AB%98%E7%9A%84%E7%A5%A8%E6%88%BF%E3%80%82%0A%0A%E8%BF%87%E5%BE%80%E7%9A%84%E7%BB%8F%E9%AA%8C%E8%AF%81%E6%98%8E%EF%BC%8C%E6%9A%91%E6%9C%9F%E6%A1%A3%E7%A1%AE%E5%AE%9E%E6%98%AF%E4%B8%80%E5%B9%B4%E4%B8%AD%E7%9A%84%E5%A5%BD%E6%97%B6%E5%80%99%E3%80%822015%20%E5%B9%B4%E5%92%8C%202017%20%E5%B9%B4%E7%9A%84%E3%80%8A%E6%8D%89%E5%A6%96%E8%AE%B0%E3%80%8B%E4%BB%A5%E5%8F%8A%E3%80%8A%E6%88%98%E7%8B%BC%202%E3%80%8B%E5%88%86%E5%88%AB%E5%88%B7%E6%96%B0%E4%BA%86%E5%BD%93%E6%97%B6%E7%9A%84%E4%B8%AD%E5%9B%BD%E7%94%B5%E5%BD%B1%E7%A5%A8%E6%88%BF%E7%BA%AA%E5%BD%95%EF%BC%8C%E8%80%8C%E6%AF%8F%E5%B9%B4%E7%9A%84%207%20%E6%9C%88%E5%92%8C%208%20%E6%9C%88%E8%B4%A1%E7%8C%AE%E7%9A%84%E5%8D%95%E6%9C%88%E7%A5%A8%E6%88%BF%E4%B9%9F%E4%BB%85%E6%AC%A1%E4%BA%8E%E6%98%A5%E8%8A%82%E6%A1%A3%E3%80%82%0A%0A%0A%0A%E5%9C%A8%E7%9B%AE%E5%89%8D%E7%94%B5%E5%BD%B1%E9%93%B6%E5%B9%95%E5%B7%B2%E7%BB%8F%E6%89%A9%E5%BC%A0%E5%88%B0%205.6%20%E4%B8%87%E5%BC%A0%E7%9A%84%E5%89%8D%E6%8F%90%E4%B8%8B%EF%BC%8C%E8%BF%99%E6%A0%B7%E7%9A%84%E5%A2%9E%E9%95%BF%E7%8E%87%E7%BB%99%E5%BD%B1%E9%99%A2%E7%BB%8F%E8%90%A5%E5%B8